      "text": "We are given the conditions for dividing the coins into different groups:\n1. When divided among six people, 4 coins are left over.\n2. When divided among five people, 3 coins are left over.\n\nWe need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) that satisfies these conditions. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nLet's start by expressing \\( N \\) in terms of the first condition:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression into the second condition:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side modulo 5, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[ k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to 4 modulo 5, we can write:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nThus, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), so:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow we need to find the remainder when 28 coins are divided among seven people:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ with a remainder of } 0 \\]\nSo the number of coins left when divided among seven people is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
