      "text": "Given the problem, we need to find the smallest number of coins \\( N \\) that satisfy two conditions:\n1. When divided by 6, it leaves a remainder of 4.\n2. When divided by 5, it leaves a remainder of 3.\n\nWe start with these congruences:\n\\[\nN \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\n\\[\nN \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\nFirst, express \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence. Since \\( N = 6k + 4 \\) for some integer \\( k \\), substitute this into the second congruence:\n\\[\n6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSimplify the congruence by reducing \\( 6k + 4 \\mod 5 \\):\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 3 - 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nSince \\( 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\), we have:\n\\[\nk \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n\\]\nThus, \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nk = 5m + 4 \\text{ for some integer } m\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[\nN = 6k + 4 = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28\n\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nN \\equiv 28 \\pmod{30}\n\\]\n\nThis means \\( N \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[\nN = 30m + 28\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive \\( N \\):\n\\[\nN = 30 \\cdot 0 + 28 = 28\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to divide 28 coins among 7 people and find the remainder:\n\\[\n28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ with a remainder of } 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when 28 coins are divided among 7 people is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
